      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:d3a8e12a48997eb8f064d7e1dbc877b20fec6f3b88de30f162a6a18aeb64aa89",
      "text": "Failure drills deliberately disrupt a workload and check behavior against explicit expectations. Deterministic simulation adds control over time, randomness and scheduling so failures can be reproduced. The evidence supports synthetic fault workloads, invariant checks, coordinated game days and conversion of discovered failures into scenario regression tests. For crypto-bot monitors, these are proposed adaptations of distributed-system testing practice. Trading backtests have narrower execution assumptions and do not alone validate operational behavior. Reproducibility can break after code changes, and mocked exchange dependencies leave a real-integration gap.",
